\label{lem:arithmetic-multiplicative-algebraic}
Let $\alpha_1,\alpha_2$ be real algebraic numbers such that
\[
1,\ \alpha_1,\ \alpha_2
\]
are linearly independent over $\mathbb Q$. Then, for every $\varepsilon > 0$, there exists a constant $c = c(\alpha_1, \alpha_2, \varepsilon) > 0$ such that
\begin{equation*}
\|q\alpha_1\| \, \|q\alpha_2\| \ge c |q|^{-1-\varepsilon}
\quad \text{for all } q \in \mathbb Z \setminus \{0\}.
\end{equation*}
